 STANDING FACTS  (things that cost time to learn — do not re-derive)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

DEFINITION OF DONE
  * THREE commands, not one. `cargo test --workspace`,
    `rust-analyzer diagnostics .` (see PROC MACROS below) and, since 0008,
    `cargo doc --no-deps --workspace`. The last one was ALREADY RED on main when
    M4 started — a broken intra-doc link in `wire.rs` that nothing in the loop
    ran rustdoc to catch. For a crate whose D1 ambition is publishing, docs.rs
    would have failed the build. And none of the three replaces opening the
    browser; see TESTING TRAPS.

TOOLCHAIN
  * `dx` and the `dioxus` crate versions must match EXACTLY or dx refuses to run.
  * Workspace is edition 2024 -> `#[no_mangle]` must be `#[unsafe(no_mangle)]`.

BUILD / SERVE
  * `dx serve --hot-patch` is BROKEN on wasm (exceptions proposal). Use plain
    `dx serve --platform web`. Incremental rebuild is ~1.5s, which is enough.
  * `spikes/s1-hotreload` is EXCLUDED from the cargo workspace. You must `cd`
    into it; `--package s1-hotreload` from the root will not find it.
  * `dx serve` does NOT pick up changes to a path dependency under `crates/`,
    and does NOT re-run build.rs when a NEW story file appears. Restart it.
    Cost measured in session 0005: a full restart-and-build is ~20-25s.
  * dx serves index.html for `/` with ANY query string, so an iframe pointed at
    `?viewMode=preview` loads the same bundle with no extra file and no config.

BROWSER / IFRAME  (session 0005)
  * `window.parent` IS `window` when a document is not framed. A preview opened
    directly would post to itself and answer its own messages, which is why the
    sender's ViewMode is on the wire and a reader only accepts its peer.
  * `window.onmessage` is a SHARED bus. Browser extensions post at it — a wallet
    extension showed up in the console on the very first run. Every message
    needs a magic prefix and everything else must be dropped unparsed.
  * A plain JS callback (no Dioxus scope, no runtime guard) CAN write a Signal
    and the owning component re-renders. Verified in spikes/s4-iframe before any
    of the real transport was written.
  * A percentage height does not resolve through the host app's mount element.
    `div#main` has auto height, so `min-height:100%` in the preview stylesheet
    collapsed. Use `100vh` — a framed stylesheet cannot know what it is inside.
  * `std::panic::set_hook` requires `Send + Sync`, which an `Rc`-based channel
    is not. A hook that captures NOTHING and looks `web_sys::window()` up afresh
    satisfies the bound. Chain the previous hook or you lose Dioxus's console
    output. The hook DOES run under `panic = "abort"` — then the module is gone.

THE VIEWPORT ADDON  (session 0007)
  * The frame's WIDTH BELONGS TO THE SHELL, so "render this story at 360px" is a
    style attribute on the iframe and nothing else. The story is not told, not
    re-rendered, and not handed a breakpoint — its own media queries fire
    because it genuinely is that size. This is the dividend from the M3 split.
  * Its three halves land on the three mechanisms already here:
      LIST      -> a declaration on Project (`with_viewports`)
      CHOICE    -> `parameters { viewport: "mobile" }`, merged innermost-first
      SELECTION -> the globals ArgMap, under the reserved names `viewport` and
                   `viewport-rotated`
    The list could NOT be a parameter: ParamValue is Str|Num|Bool and a viewport
    is a record. Widening it would have been a fourth vocabulary.
  * `responsive` is a REAL VALUE, not an absence, because absence already means
    "whatever this story asked for". `viewport::responsive_needs_saying` is what
    keeps it out of links where it would mean nothing.
  * `viewport::resolve` is called by BOTH halves — the manager to size the frame,
    the preview to fill in StoryContext::viewport. Same reason `landing_story` is
    shared: two implementations of one rule will drift.

AUTODOCS  (session 0008)
  * A DOCS PAGE IS AN ID, NOT A MODE. `kebab(title) + "--docs"` lives in the same
    id space a story does, so it needs no URL parameter, no wire message and no
    second transport. M4's diff to `wire.rs` is EMPTY. `docs::entry` resolves an
    id to `Entry::Story` or `Entry::Docs`, asking the registry FIRST — because
    `--docs` is a legal story id and a story named "Docs" must win the collision.
  * IT RENDERS IN THE PREVIEW, and could not be anywhere else. Every example on
    it is a real story through `render_decorated`, which needs a live scope, the
    author's decorators, and the preview's CSS cascade. A page drawn by the
    manager would be components styled by the workbench.
  * `Span::source_text()` GIVES THE CODE AS WRITTEN.
    `block.brace_token.span.join().source_text()` returns the exact bytes —
    comments, line breaks, indentation. `quote!(#block).to_string()` gives
    `ButtonProps { variant : .. , .. base () }`, a rendering of the tokens.
    `Span::call_site()` gives only the attribute (`#[story]`), and the block's
    *open* delimiter alone gives `{`; the JOIN is what makes it work.
    `DelimSpan::join()` is available on stable through proc-macro2. `None` means
    the item came out of another macro — fall back to the token stream.
  * `100vh` IN A DECORATOR HAS TWO RIGHT ANSWERS. On the canvas the story owns
    the document and 100vh is the frame; on a docs page a dozen examples stack
    and each becomes a screen tall. No CSS fixes it — `vh` is always the viewport
    and no containing block redefines it. `StoryContext::view()` is how a
    decorator is told. Storybook has the same footgun and no better answer.
  * A DOCS EXAMPLE'S ARGS HAVE NOWHERE TO GO. `use_args` writes back through the
    manager, whose args belong to the SELECTED ENTRY — on a docs page, the page.
    Gating it off would make a controlled story inert, so `StoryHost` keeps a
    local overlay when `reporting` is false.
  * A DOC COMMENT IS RUSTDOC, AND RUSTDOC IS MARKDOWN. The macro captures only
    the SUMMARY PARAGRAPH (rustdoc's own short-description rule) and
    `ui/src/inline.rs` renders the three inline constructs that actually occur —
    code spans, emphasis, links-reduced-to-their-label. Quoting raw puts
    `` `oninput` `` and `**bold**` on the page verbatim, which reads as a typo.
  * A DESCRIPTION HAS TWO SOURCES because a proc macro cannot see the COMPONENT
    FUNCTION's doc comment — it only sees the tokens it was invoked on. So
    `story_meta! { description }` is explicit, and `Controllable::DOCS` (the
    props type's `///`, captured by the derive) is the fallback. The fallback is
    the useful one: it documents a component with no storybook-specific prose.
    `DOCS` is an associated const WITH A DEFAULT, so adding it to `Controllable`
    broke no hand-written impl — unlike `wire_actions` in 0004.

VALUE VOCABULARIES  (there are three, and there must not be a fourth)
  * args      change at run time, scoped to ONE STORY, declared by the props type
  * parameters are STATIC, scoped to a level (project/component/story), merged
              innermost-wins by ResolvedParameters
  * globals   change at run time, scoped to THE WHOLE BOOK, declared on Project
  Globals are declared with the SAME `Control` enum a prop uses and carried in
  an `ArgMap`, which is what gave them a widget, a URL encoding and a wire
  encoding with no new code. Do not grow a fourth. (LOG-0006 / G1.)
  * THE URL CODEC IS UNTYPED, BY DESIGN. `theme:dark` decodes as
    ArgValue::Text, never Variant; `scale:2` as Num even for a String field.
    Typing is restored on the way OUT — by the generated applier for args, by
    `GlobalType::coerce` for globals. Anything that compares a value against a
    typed ArgValue must coerce first, or it works when clicked and fails when
    pasted. (LOG-0006 / G2.)

RUST / DIOXUS GOTCHAS
  * `use_effect` DOES NOT RUN under a bare `VirtualDom`. `rebuild_in_place` and
    `render_immediate_to_vec` never fire one; `VirtualDom::process_events()`
    does, but only once there are no dirty scopes. A headless test pass is
    therefore render -> process_events -> deliver. This is not just a test
    detail: it is why the preview's `PreviewReady` handshake is a `use_hook`
    placed after its `subscribe` hook, not an effect. Ordering WITHIN a
    component is enough and needs no renderer.
  * `key` is ONLY consulted when diffing a LIST (`diff_keyed_children`). A lone
    keyed child in a fixed template position goes through `diff_node`, which
    never looks at it — so a changing key there does NOT remount. To force a
    remount, render a keyed list of exactly one. This is why the preview wraps
    `StoryHost` in `for def in [def]`.
  * Story bodies must NOT be rendered inline in a shared scope. Their hooks land
    in that scope's hook list, and two stories with different hook counts then
    corrupt each other on a switch. Each story gets its own remounted scope.
  * `Callback`/`EventHandler` `PartialEq` is pointer identity, and `Callback::new`
    allocates in the CURRENT SCOPE'S owner — freed only when that scope drops.
    Building one per render is legal but accumulates. See LOG-0004.
  * `try_consume_context` panics with no RUNTIME (not just no context): it
    unwraps `Runtime::current()`. Use `Runtime::try_current()` first if the call
    site has to be total. `has_context` has the same trap.
  * rsx! format strings interpolate IDENTS ONLY. `{a * b}` and `{if x {..}}`
    fail to parse. Compute into a `let` first.
  * `rsx!` and `EventHandler::new` need an active Dioxus SCOPE, not just a
    runtime. Use `VirtualDom::in_scope(ScopeId::ROOT, ..)`, not `in_runtime`.
    This covers ANYTHING that evaluates a story's props, not just rendering —
    `StoryDef::base_args()` panics outside a scope too.
  * `rsx!` expands to UNQUALIFIED `dioxus_core::` / `dioxus_signals::` paths.
    They resolve only because `dioxus::prelude` re-exports those crate names, so
    any file invoking rsx! — including one where OUR macro bridge expands it —
    needs `dioxus::prelude::*` in scope. `dioxus_storybook::prelude` re-exports
    it for this reason. Two globs re-exporting the same items do not conflict.
  * `Key` is at `dioxus::prelude::Key` (via dioxus_elements). There is no
    `dioxus::events::Key`.
  * Writing a `Signal` inside an `Fn` callback: copy it INSIDE the closure body
    (`let mut s = s;`). Capturing a `mut` copy makes the closure `FnMut`, even
    though `Signal` is `Copy`, because the binding is what gets mutated.
  * Rust privacy runs downward only. A build-script-generated registry module is
    an ANCESTOR of the story modules it references, so generated story statics
    must be `pub` regardless of the story fn's own visibility.

PROC MACROS vs RUST-ANALYZER  (cost a session to find; cargo test cannot catch it)
  * EDITING A PROC MACRO'S OUTPUT REQUIRES RESTARTING RUST-ANALYZER. The editor
    keeps the OLD proc-macro dylib while type-checking against the NEW trait, so
    adding a required method to a derived trait shows a false
    `E0046: not all trait items implemented` at EVERY derive site until the
    server reloads. Hit in session 0004 after `Controllable` gained
    `wire_actions`. Confirm with `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` and
    headless `rust-analyzer diagnostics .` — if both are clean, it is staleness,
    not your code. Fix: "rust-analyzer: Restart Server" / reload the window.
  * Adding a REQUIRED method to a public trait breaks hand-written impls. That is
    a deliberate accepted cost for `Controllable`, which is derive-only by
    documentation and has not been published yet. Revisit before 0.1.0 ships.
  * NEVER have one proc macro emit a `macro_rules!` that another proc macro's
    expansion then calls with a VALUE argument. rustc accepts it; rust-analyzer
    reports a false `E0425: no such value in this scope` at every call site.
    Closest upstream issue: rust-analyzer#10644, OPEN since 2021. Do not wait
    for a fix — emit a plain `fn` and have the macro learn the type it needs.
  * What DOES work across an expansion boundary: referencing a plain module
    ITEM (a `const`, a `fn`) generated by another proc macro. Verified.
  * This is NOT the common "proc macro library has no proc macros" error. That
    one means the dylib failed to load; the generic advice for it (enable
    procMacro, cargo clean, restart server) does not apply here. Ours loaded
    fine — derives expanded, only the macro_rules bridge failed.
  * `rust-analyzer diagnostics .` runs headless and is part of DEFINITION OF
    DONE for anything macro-facing. A green `cargo test` is not evidence that
    the authoring surface is usable in an editor.
  * RA also emits false `E0282: type annotations needed` for `Weak::upgrade`
    results and for tuple-destructuring closure params over `Rc<dyn Fn(..)>`.
    Write the type out; do not chase it.

PROJECT STRUCTURE
  * dx builds a workspace MEMBER fine — s1-hotreload's exclusion was not about
    dx disliking workspaces. `examples/button-gallery` is a member and builds.
  * `[profile.wasm-dev] panic = "abort"` is only honoured in the WORKSPACE ROOT
    manifest. In a member package Cargo ignores it.
  * `#[non_exhaustive]` has no effect inside its own crate — our own matches
    stay exhaustive, and a wildcard arm there is an unreachable-pattern warning.

TESTING TRAPS (all produced convincing WRONG answers)
  * LTO const-folds a static registry into a literal. A registry test that just
    counts entries can pass while proving nothing. Use `black_box` and read real
    string bytes out of linear memory.
  * macOS `sed -i ''` writes a sibling temp file (`.!PID!name.rs`). dx's file
    watcher latches onto THAT, not your file. Never use it to script an edit for
    a hot-reload measurement; write in place instead.
  * A HEADLESS VirtualDom CANNOT DELIVER A CLICK EITHER. In 0008 the
    Canvas/Docs toggle sat before the viewport picker, which is hidden on a docs
    page — so clicking "Docs" made the picker vanish and the toggle slide out
    from under the cursor. 195 tests were green. The user reported it from the
    browser within minutes. Fix: the toolbar is a `1fr auto 1fr` GRID — crumb
    left, story id exactly centred, tools right with the toggle last against the
    edge — so only what is to the LEFT of the toggle ever changes. A flex row
    with a spacer cannot promise the centre column stays centred.
  * A HEADLESS VirtualDom CANNOT DELIVER A KEYSTROKE. Everything keyboard-shaped
    in the shell — the sidebar's arrow navigation, `/` for search, and the fact
    that a focused toolbar `<select>` was having its arrow keys stolen by that
    navigation until 0007 — is untested and untestable at this layer. Open the
    browser. (LOG-0007 / F4.)
  * A substring assertion can pass for the wrong reason. TWICE NOW.
      - `story_bodies_are_invoked_in_scope` asserted the shell contained "ok",
        and "dioxus-storybook" contains "ok" (LOG-0005 / F7).
      - Two toolbar tests asserted on "dxsb-globals", which the manager's
        INLINED STYLESHEET names whether the toolbar rendered or not
        (LOG-0006 / G4).
    Assert on markup that cannot appear by accident: `class="dxsb-globals"`,
    not `dxsb-globals`. Remember the manager document contains all of
    MANAGER_CSS as text.
  * A test whose premise is "this does not exist yet" can quietly become its
    opposite. A wire test used the tag "globals" as an example of an UNKNOWN
    tag; one session later `globals` was real. Name such placeholders so they
    can never become valid — `no-such-tag-will-ever-exist`. (LOG-0006 / G5.)


